 ## Proof
 
-1.1 Choose a small surface disk about $z_i$ meeting no other mark or attaching curve. The witnesses in [F1] are in the standing smooth embedded-disk category, including ordinary smooth boundary at a tangential mark. Choose their finitely many parametrizations and apply [F6], supported near this mark and away from the compression disks. This is an allowed arc normalization in the FIXED splitting: it retains the old surface, all old curves and marks, the side assignments and simultaneous witnesses, and preserves the oriented knot type by an actual ambient knot isotopy. Its new knot is smooth and transverse at the mark. Choose a smaller coordinate ball meeting only that knot interval. Write the surface as $v=0$, with $U_\alpha$ on $v\ge0$ and $U_\beta$ on $v\le0$. Transversality gives its tangential coordinates as smooth functions of $v$ by the local inverse theorem. Subtract those functions from the two tangential coordinates, and then add $-v/c$ to the first one for a fixed $c>0$. This is an invertible triangular coordinate change preserving the surface, and makes the ONE smooth oriented knot have $y=0$, $v=-cx$, traversed with increasing $x$. A sign change of the remaining tangential coordinate fixes the ambient orientation if necessary. The old $z_i$ is the origin. Thus the two halves match because they are restrictions of the actual normalized smooth knot, not merely because their endpoints agree. The normalization and these coordinate choices can be confined to the chosen mark neighborhood, leaving every other datum fixed. [F1, F2, F5, F6, F7, given, construct]
